[{:db/id "q-143876/selection/b7cb6413c3b2f55930886db8d563769bc3a0000b15b5ad93305be24fe4f3fbab" :answer/id #uuid "c30e77af-1243-526a-9dd2-9fac58e17d39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{18}{55}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143876/selection/64a81d00fcec02eb3c52e4baa9e59c42f82593b6b9632d6a21d91b48cc003a41" :answer/id #uuid "0f6578c2-3e2a-59ca-9395-cf95e3481042" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{50}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143876/selection/a16328a92bb6ccb93fc14e521721ea4c1e42c97e908d61bbcb23027fb0a11ce9" :answer/id #uuid "2e97dc51-6646-5265-ace0-e86a9d3b1bb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{23}{60}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143876/selection/ed1c82e0d20aaa3134847fddeaf7006dc5e5abaf49f3e4ed4baac44ef6ac5a96" :answer/id #uuid "6adb2792-0256-5852-a7e2-ec880e34fbd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{62}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143876/selection/50ceb2d79c8de272c999a461548898a4816663c4b23ce776d0b7c90bd7fb13cf" :answer/id #uuid "2480cdbb-3eea-5c96-bb8f-edcdbea7e886" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{24}{55}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-143876/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac69e96d-fec1-590b-97ef-264b252bbc4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-143876/selection/a16328a92bb6ccb93fc14e521721ea4c1e42c97e908d61bbcb23027fb0a11ce9" :answer-field/choices ["q-143876/selection/b7cb6413c3b2f55930886db8d563769bc3a0000b15b5ad93305be24fe4f3fbab" "q-143876/selection/64a81d00fcec02eb3c52e4baa9e59c42f82593b6b9632d6a21d91b48cc003a41" "q-143876/selection/a16328a92bb6ccb93fc14e521721ea4c1e42c97e908d61bbcb23027fb0a11ce9" "q-143876/selection/ed1c82e0d20aaa3134847fddeaf7006dc5e5abaf49f3e4ed4baac44ef6ac5a96" "q-143876/selection/50ceb2d79c8de272c999a461548898a4816663c4b23ce776d0b7c90bd7fb13cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-143876" :question/id #uuid "4fc2267c-cf00-5edb-8fcf-dca3892e059b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143876" :question/problem "Three baskets, $A,B,$ and $C,$ contain pears. It is known that $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the pears from basket $A$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ of those from basket $B$ are ripe. A basket is selected at random, and a pear is chosen at random from the basket. If the probability that the selected pear is ripe is $\\frac{1}{4},$  what proportion of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that the pear is ripe, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the pear comes from basket $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a ripe pear, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap B)+P(R\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a basket is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from baskets $A$ and $B$ are $\\frac{1}{6}$ and $\\frac{1}{5},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{6},\\,P(R|B)=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\n- Since the probability of picking a ripe pear from one of the baskets is $\\frac{1}{4},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(R|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|B)P(B)+P(R|C)P(C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =(\\frac{1}{6})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{5})(\\frac{1}{3})+P(R|C)(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{15}+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{18}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\cdot P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{180} \\\\ P(R|C) & =\\frac{23}{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{23}{60}$ of the pears from basket $C$ are ripe." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-143876/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-143876"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-127319/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "4f7a71c8-2717-5dce-8fd2-f0246d31c46e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127319/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a1dfe140-fa66-5a20-8ca7-81a106a9005b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127319/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "85d04224-86f0-5523-9d5e-be8017437461" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127319/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "bd5289b5-b1e7-52cb-bb1d-a62a362a4647" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127319/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "037f2a02-949f-5dfc-893e-9e985cfb18b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-127319/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8d74619b-474d-5689-a782-c6516775daf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-127319/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-127319/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-127319/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-127319/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-127319/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-127319/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-127319" :question/id #uuid "6bc59286-96e6-53b4-9925-d6577fe6687e" :question/math-academy-id "q-127319" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,0,1⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨0,-1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨2,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩=⟨2,1,0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =i \\\\  & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨2,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,0⟩| \\\\  & =|2+0+0| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-127319/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2a445fa-d44b-574a-8369-b30cac3a3c96"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-127319"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-33732"] :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the origin and the osculating plane for the curve $r(t)$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,4t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ T(t) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t,\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t⟩ \\\\ N(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ and $N$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal, respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the unit tangent and the principal normal at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩ \\\\ N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, the position vector of the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is\n\n$$\np=r(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,4(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})⟩=⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find a vector that's perpendicular to the osculating plane. To do that, we use the cross product of the unit tangent $T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and the principal normal $N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =T(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\times N(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}⟨0,4,3⟩\\times ⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,j-4\\,k) \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance between our plane and the origin is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{|(p-0)\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=\\frac{|p\\cdot n|}{‖n‖}=|p\\cdot n|,\n$$\n\nsince $‖n‖=1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|p\\cdot n| \\\\  & =|⟨-1,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{3}{5},-\\frac{4}{5}⟩| \\\\  & =|\\,0+\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}+0\\,| \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-337570"] :question/worked-solution "Orthogonal trajectories are families of curves that intersect another given family of curves at right angles. In other words, the tangent lines of curves from each family are perpendicular at every intersection point.\n\nLet's determine the slope of the family of curves given by $xy=C.$ First, we implicitly differentiate with respect to $x$ and solve for the derivative of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}(xy) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(C) \\\\ y+x{y}^{′} & =0 \\\\ x{y}^{′} & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =-\\frac{y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the parameter $C$ has already been eliminated.\n\nFor a curve to intersect this family orthogonally, its slope ${y}_{⊥}^{′}$ must be the negative reciprocal of the slope ${y}^{′}$ above. Hence,\n\n$$\n{y}_{⊥}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{y}{x})}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nHence, the orthogonal trajectories must satisfy\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nTo find the general solution, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} & =x \\\\ \\int y\\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}\\,dx & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\int y\\,dy & =\\int x\\,dx \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{K}_{1} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=2{K}_{1}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal trajectories of the family of curves $xy=C,$ where $C$ is a parameter, are given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+K,\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is a parameter. Some curves from each family for specific parameter values are given in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/98159dfb60fd280468ad8165475b5ba58e41658174a932b4c9555ef1153a577c.png)\n\n**Note**: For the slopes ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}_{⊥}^{′},$ we divide by $x$ and $y,$ respectively. So, the orthogonal trajectories are only defined on the region $x,y\\ne 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335282/selection/efb176a941580f78b5f55946537a36759297b67e63021a37ab2228278f218c37" :answer/id #uuid "f558a2c9-9cf1-5bef-90d3-9a12cde26dab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335282/selection/cc9a4cf732860f12e7ef5c55412d3757242881f0728d1d6f1be2d56fca388947" :answer/id #uuid "4074a504-6e17-5bd5-80dc-58b7e403bf1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ minute" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335282/selection/57371beaa639b9ba099779b685b8739ba03364b1939a9dc42e7a38a4831e3c1b" :answer/id #uuid "556df5ed-b540-58fd-b44a-adb7ddad3eb2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335282/selection/ff0ea8d422f6fb1361d426da0b8247a6341aa486d495313c63d0bc9a8f400d2b" :answer/id #uuid "ef756984-575a-5523-8188-1244943c4e9e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335282/selection/d7130ff75532cfefd05b547deb232301356b851a1507c781ce9ad92918120866" :answer/id #uuid "a74401ea-309f-5294-b052-678f1a681ce2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2$ minutes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335282/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdb18c16-9cae-5b03-a649-2f26b297d661" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335282/selection/cc9a4cf732860f12e7ef5c55412d3757242881f0728d1d6f1be2d56fca388947" :answer-field/choices ["q-335282/selection/efb176a941580f78b5f55946537a36759297b67e63021a37ab2228278f218c37" "q-335282/selection/cc9a4cf732860f12e7ef5c55412d3757242881f0728d1d6f1be2d56fca388947" "q-335282/selection/57371beaa639b9ba099779b685b8739ba03364b1939a9dc42e7a38a4831e3c1b" "q-335282/selection/ff0ea8d422f6fb1361d426da0b8247a6341aa486d495313c63d0bc9a8f400d2b" "q-335282/selection/d7130ff75532cfefd05b547deb232301356b851a1507c781ce9ad92918120866"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335282" :question/id #uuid "765540e0-6bf9-5c09-a934-b0d2b992dba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-335282" :question/problem "The volume $X(t)$ (in $\\text{L}$) of water in a container as it fills toward its maximum capacity can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial volume is $1.5\\,\\text{L},$ how long does it take for the container to reach a volume of $2\\,\\text{L}?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the container's volume is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dX}{dt}=0.4(3-X).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dX}{3-X} & =0.4\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dX}{3-X} & =\\int 0.4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3-X| & =0.4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $3-X>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $X(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-X & ={e}^{-0.4t-C} \\\\ 3-X & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ 3-X & =K{e}^{-0.4t} \\\\ X & =3-K{e}^{-0.4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial volume was $1.5\\,\\text{L}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $X(0)=1.5,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5 & =3-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 1.5 & =3-K \\\\ K & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nX(t)=3-1.5{e}^{-0.4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the volume is $2\\,\\text{L}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X({t}_{1}) & =2 \\\\ 3-1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =2 \\\\ 1 & =1.5{e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-0.4{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -0.4{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{0.4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =1.013… \\\\  & ≈1\\,\\text{minute},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335282/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335282"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65145/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "7ddafe21-3567-526b-b2ae-185a6f8c0bec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65145/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2814d360-7194-570f-9f65-58f8bb9ca8cb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65145/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "7424d9fe-e76a-5a0e-b498-ea5cfa88774b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65145/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "06fce26b-7020-5f61-9971-36933a80e252" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65145/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c8aa9617-31a4-53fc-8878-4b3f76800e82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65145/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "02a4ddfa-5f0b-5d3d-9cb6-b34db3ad5044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-65145/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-65145/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-65145/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-65145/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-65145/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-65145/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65145" :question/id #uuid "b171da41-b950-5445-bd40-70968beeadd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65145" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $0$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0+0}{3\\cdot 0+0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x+x}{3x+x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3x}{4x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-2x+x}{3x-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-x}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-65145/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65145"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79811/selection/9b32bd1c2d85515eb67bf6ed1aa42ff7a66b88544eaafce7b11ad994fbde1d11" :answer/id #uuid "320ddc7c-7c7d-541e-9ebe-9ac46df72c2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79811/selection/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" :answer/id #uuid "e3d1a48d-b303-5108-bf3d-41cf2b142017" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79811/selection/98825339913b96286f9751ca67affc1b40dbfb36f8a6273cb27701f4b3f223de" :answer/id #uuid "3e591d12-74c2-5577-b196-484985280fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79811/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "542a575b-c995-5c99-8b0f-3f774e748289" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79811/selection/a39e4c3855e119cc134cd501616b2d4242dd33fe6134da1d7da0776d673411f7" :answer/id #uuid "2f8f7c53-d3cc-5bfe-9343-72ec3b72a943" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79811/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d173091-98b1-5f9e-ba14-223fe159f379" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79811/selection/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" :answer-field/choices ["q-79811/selection/9b32bd1c2d85515eb67bf6ed1aa42ff7a66b88544eaafce7b11ad994fbde1d11" "q-79811/selection/95a1d5fa369c4d08bda10228102d0fbae8f93732afc4c2ec3773c4f3d47df28c" "q-79811/selection/98825339913b96286f9751ca67affc1b40dbfb36f8a6273cb27701f4b3f223de" "q-79811/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-79811/selection/a39e4c3855e119cc134cd501616b2d4242dd33fe6134da1d7da0776d673411f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79811" :question/id #uuid "cfde08b6-0b33-56dc-ab2e-672049a30b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79811" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y)$ from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2x\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(0.5,1),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(0.5,1) & =2\\cdot 0.5\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(0.5+Δx,1+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(0.5,1)$ to $(0,2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-0.5=-0.5, \\\\ Δy & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈1\\,Δx+1\\,Δy \\\\  & ≈1\\cdot (-0.5)+1\\cdot (1) \\\\  & ≈0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y)$ increases by approximately $0.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79811/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab023be2-0156-5de6-8843-5b720775d649"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79811"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81740/selection/de7eb5d9cfbfd2359608bb079f4c4ab4dcf1b95c4cf9a56d9bb367e0dde8fab4" :answer/id #uuid "51a8a8d3-3021-5096-952b-ca349320a568" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+x+y+z+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81740/selection/61278046d680c333494142b1c58974a3ae68beb4491a283c63fdbc266ea2fff8" :answer/id #uuid "37c2d898-6a51-5075-9c1a-67842a6be680" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+x+y+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81740/selection/be6643b4c96e21c27a22c6e16b330944a801bebf28839319571ca475968c8bcb" :answer/id #uuid "dfd4134d-765e-586f-851e-78f013e6d97a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz+y+z+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81740/selection/0d1689acbb58737c90a2ea95297c1ef29eb9d0e033d53734e1a33148d1d5f256" :answer/id #uuid "db422412-128c-551d-bfe7-009fa0d0a988" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz+xz+xy+yz+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81740/selection/a2647e48925b5f06157165e1cd9c635e5db1009480330a89ab0e381a44236fa9" :answer/id #uuid "28f0ba0d-36db-5319-9d3d-a60c9cdd8b53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81740/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "333026f2-f23d-583e-938d-a19470e048a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81740/selection/a2647e48925b5f06157165e1cd9c635e5db1009480330a89ab0e381a44236fa9" :answer-field/choices ["q-81740/selection/de7eb5d9cfbfd2359608bb079f4c4ab4dcf1b95c4cf9a56d9bb367e0dde8fab4" "q-81740/selection/61278046d680c333494142b1c58974a3ae68beb4491a283c63fdbc266ea2fff8" "q-81740/selection/be6643b4c96e21c27a22c6e16b330944a801bebf28839319571ca475968c8bcb" "q-81740/selection/0d1689acbb58737c90a2ea95297c1ef29eb9d0e033d53734e1a33148d1d5f256" "q-81740/selection/a2647e48925b5f06157165e1cd9c635e5db1009480330a89ab0e381a44236fa9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81740" :question/id #uuid "7429fa1e-a8b3-5b4c-87be-75aad7649b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81740" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(y+1)\\,i+(x+z+1)\\,j+(y+1)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xy+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x+z+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =z+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (z+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =yz+y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=yz+y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+x+yz+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =y+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =1 \\\\ h(z) & =z+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+yz+x+y+z+C.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-81740/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81740"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140842/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "d12ea080-cff9-5044-ac40-ef37c623517c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140842/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "fe147d5c-ed6c-55f7-8a2f-0f6524262929" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140842/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "c7dc72ae-b933-5509-95b6-26b3ca2355ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140842/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "3f32c1ca-5ea4-5bc6-9eef-a9a7617398aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140842/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "cd71f260-59ae-5c57-be24-c4d8d32e148b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140842/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "266295c1-cb88-5e0b-a120-392a3af6ca3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140842/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-140842/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-140842/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-140842/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-140842/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-140842/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140842" :question/id #uuid "499f8d57-6ade-5414-a50c-fa707a48dd35" :question/math-academy-id "q-140842" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+8I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140842/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140842"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-38540/selection/9f636376e534757c5f2f3212a593d476f453e623359c6327a58493509985e6ec" :answer/id #uuid "42c3c456-cf9f-513c-9898-92864d99b1c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38540/selection/0ff41ea715308e83bb30fde65194453ba86e05eaca14fa892c68e2a7e6f523fc" :answer/id #uuid "aa3cf698-10b5-52fd-90c8-078f90164003" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38540/selection/d26e6dca036b9c7c97252e5f41a7b396adf9159577b5198f411400973f614d64" :answer/id #uuid "49086f4c-a404-5e64-84cc-cc41a883c591" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38540/selection/b84040474533a4084ff6843b2ac3048d14b6ac2d4439a9746c8af34131ec321f" :answer/id #uuid "beb87bdf-8bde-5622-915a-0064157dbb99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38540/selection/a562f41b1567481b10635fc1dda8b5c24dcb86cded5e426a669dc2904a412a18" :answer/id #uuid "26a2b8f6-9638-5b35-99d0-eac09f89ccfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-38540/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1accf33e-bbe4-520e-8bdd-0f6f5f73bcaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-38540/selection/9f636376e534757c5f2f3212a593d476f453e623359c6327a58493509985e6ec" :answer-field/choices ["q-38540/selection/9f636376e534757c5f2f3212a593d476f453e623359c6327a58493509985e6ec" "q-38540/selection/0ff41ea715308e83bb30fde65194453ba86e05eaca14fa892c68e2a7e6f523fc" "q-38540/selection/d26e6dca036b9c7c97252e5f41a7b396adf9159577b5198f411400973f614d64" "q-38540/selection/b84040474533a4084ff6843b2ac3048d14b6ac2d4439a9746c8af34131ec321f" "q-38540/selection/a562f41b1567481b10635fc1dda8b5c24dcb86cded5e426a669dc2904a412a18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-38540" :question/id #uuid "b03e0038-023a-5069-84d1-998d6d535a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-38540" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ (shown above) and a basis $C$ of ${R}^{3}.$\nThe matrix $[T]_{B|C}$ of the linear map $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to these bases, and the vector $x\\in {R}^{2},$ are also given. Find $[T(x)]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the coordinates of the image of $x$ under $T$ relative to the basis $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $[x]_{B},$ the coordinates of $x$ relative to $B.$ So, let\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives us the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now row-reduce the corresponding augmented matrix using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{C} & =[T]_{B|C}\\cdot [x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-38540/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8725caed-481c-5fbe-a0c4-cffdcb308881"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-38540"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80778/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "1e895897-5109-50c8-9869-355a5ce0aea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80778/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "0a46a3bc-9089-599a-a63f-9fc68a7eeeb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80778/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "ba8d3569-d110-5d71-8155-d4acf556257c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80778/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "e312aa09-d16e-5084-a55f-f0d28e042abf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80778/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "38718a47-8236-522f-8bab-75d4a7aed904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80778/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b31f61a-2eb7-5caa-8974-71cfeeb1b2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80778/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-80778/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-80778/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-80778/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-80778/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-80778/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80778" :question/id #uuid "229858c7-5c8f-5030-8583-21587b3271c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80778" :question/problem "Consider the surface ${2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}=2.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,-1,1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{c}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=2.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy})k \\\\  & =({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)i+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)j+({2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\cdot 6z\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)k \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(y\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }i+x\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }j+3z\\,\\text{ }{2}^{3{z}^{2}+2xy}\\text{ }k) \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,1) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2((-1)\\cdot \\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }i+1\\cdot \\,\\text{ }{2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }j+3(1)\\cdot {2}^{3(1)^{2}+2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-{2}^{1}\\text{ }i+{2}^{1}\\text{ }j+3\\cdot {2}^{1}\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(-i+j+3\\text{ }k) \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,\\,12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{c}{b}=\\frac{12\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80778/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80778"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-86471/selection/a1b2ccfba458a845fd8ee10e6daa31506496a50a50042e082232a7d8cf1de62a" :answer/id #uuid "1607a6b1-fbb7-555b-9281-d7382dc69494" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86471/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "824a6509-df8e-5766-b650-e72371d6b7d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86471/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "d047b765-74ce-5fad-ae09-fcf4764f9d78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86471/selection/0d474ca756c76f6ecddcd5fcfb411f0292ef0ce725af778ee27af0d5dc8595bc" :answer/id #uuid "b804cd3b-2565-5b06-9eb6-ef89ff621a63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86471/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "7713ced6-5f50-561f-b97b-b1226413d7d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-86471/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c05fc4c1-d7d4-542c-ad70-48a1a0bcff8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-86471/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-86471/selection/a1b2ccfba458a845fd8ee10e6daa31506496a50a50042e082232a7d8cf1de62a" "q-86471/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-86471/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-86471/selection/0d474ca756c76f6ecddcd5fcfb411f0292ef0ce725af778ee27af0d5dc8595bc" "q-86471/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-86471" :question/id #uuid "d36da64c-0744-5c6d-adbb-e4cbe16d0433" :question/math-academy-id "q-86471" :question/problem "Consider the surface ${x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}=4.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,-1,0),$ what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})k \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{4})i+(4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+{z}^{4}{e}^{yz})j+({z}^{2}{e}^{yz}(yz+3))k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,0) & =2(2)(-1)^{4}\\text{ }i+[4(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}+{0}^{4}{e}^{(-1)(0)}]\\text{ }j+[{0}^{2}{e}^{(-1)(0)}((-1)(0)+3)]\\text{ }k \\\\  & =4\\text{ }i-16\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,-16,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨4,\\,-16,\\,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-16}{4}=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-86471/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "16f4bd05-1f30-576c-a538-a0ac4d533b7a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-86471"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82338/selection/3c021177a833f9f48746363d6b3329939e31d5510546a82f99495c6b82771983" :answer/id #uuid "6789d9af-6700-51dc-b2d2-5bbe7ed0b42d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82338/selection/78e985fd992d4bd5cbc51f46f1febe91d80dc29c48a8c4e1164356fb365a051f" :answer/id #uuid "46cb55d5-8a50-5539-a5ab-94381bd4a411" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82338/selection/48f9ef02653c641bc89d83d76ec52d3ef8587056cd689a43ea0a79c0f45a1451" :answer/id #uuid "3fa40dda-3ced-5113-98cd-a86d6a837bcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82338/selection/45e763ae150abb70f71da12c75d2bd8cc95e6e0c34e3d5e5d95fe4f9c230c692" :answer/id #uuid "e97ce313-8c83-56a3-8144-1a2d40385944" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{y+2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82338/selection/5ef8b075c1fb648227bed61191d93f65c6314af55bee7f022b671229f0c3ca05" :answer/id #uuid "07d44a2c-c9ca-59f6-b2b3-9b6d27a7ff0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{y+2}{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82338/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c87e4d0f-6e2a-5ad3-977f-9b55fe24dd26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-82338/selection/48f9ef02653c641bc89d83d76ec52d3ef8587056cd689a43ea0a79c0f45a1451" :answer-field/choices ["q-82338/selection/3c021177a833f9f48746363d6b3329939e31d5510546a82f99495c6b82771983" "q-82338/selection/78e985fd992d4bd5cbc51f46f1febe91d80dc29c48a8c4e1164356fb365a051f" "q-82338/selection/48f9ef02653c641bc89d83d76ec52d3ef8587056cd689a43ea0a79c0f45a1451" "q-82338/selection/45e763ae150abb70f71da12c75d2bd8cc95e6e0c34e3d5e5d95fe4f9c230c692" "q-82338/selection/5ef8b075c1fb648227bed61191d93f65c6314af55bee7f022b671229f0c3ca05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-82338" :question/id #uuid "df7a9928-b595-5d80-8b20-2abd529873c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-82338" :question/problem "Change the order of integration in the double integral \n${\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/75f9c68145ed74a2c3530012061eb65cd93bae38d82fa0d34e5dc042f8073841.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It is a type I region, and its type I representation is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le x-2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x-2\\,⟹\\,x=y+2\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type II region. The type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,y-2\\le x\\le 4},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/575e7e482f88635f6267405f66857a5488dac79833fce16d3d998df19e24d119.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{x-2}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y+2}^{4}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-82338/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb8c80f2-6dcd-568e-812b-91d39a40119c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82338"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94377/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5df26e7f-a36a-52de-9d2f-769e31017ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94377/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "498192d5-ad48-5291-aab2-3b51e4c58401" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94377/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "86d403b6-3b13-5ebd-b33c-5fbba101e04c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94377/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "a485039c-7553-52e4-9f8d-b01f2b27c8ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94377/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "84e535f8-5cf3-50df-ae2d-852b3ca8abe7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94377/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8798d2d1-2c90-5c9f-8a4f-a8a92aba57df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94377/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-94377/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94377/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-94377/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-94377/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94377/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94377" :question/id #uuid "d9246e15-4875-5686-8139-62f5acf7f760" :question/math-academy-id "q-94377" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The position of the particle $y(t)$ at time $t$ is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+16y=32t.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be used to represent the general solution for $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+16t$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+8t$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "The general solution is the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={y}_{c}(t)+{y}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(t),$ which is the solution to the simple harmonic motion equation ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.$ It can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t$\n\nIn our case\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=16\\,⟹\\,\\omega =4.\n$$\n\nThus, we have:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$\n\nNext, we find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(t).$ Since the external force is a linear function, we assume that the particular solution is also a linear function,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\alpha t+\\beta .\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution into the differential equation and equating coefficients, we find that $\\alpha =2$ and $\\beta =0.$ So, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t+ϕ)+2t, \\\\ y(t) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly II matches the forms above. Therefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-94377/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "813ec1d4-93ec-5a9e-996f-b2e672714927"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94377"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98525/selection/6193263a58aa2143a42fb9eb213c06ab78c2e89699fb0ee0761789e409f1b3f7" :answer/id #uuid "e74c42b7-d4a4-5ebd-ae89-0b7846a93317" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98525/selection/feb719f09513e76a7ba6b8a379ebf4507d684c9bf6b01efc8b3d986d8a9bd326" :answer/id #uuid "9953192b-24ce-5a1e-8f27-04b379848220" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/c5e335ffe5f2343671d1f9eca6cedc34ef34a9700837451e963cfcfe1cd153d5.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98525/selection/4813f5e1a1ef806016d68dc3b91f32b447fabce2bb8cf28e19c337b0d2228395" :answer/id #uuid "96e98df2-7173-5032-9187-e75a8fcc7098" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/a77fcb19143b7bdee347993a1da4fb6f73c5c1460c55ecab24ffde9afe7f6cf0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98525/selection/54630732ebc6196df1a383656549cfed9cd9807944e20428ce42c8367f632cb7" :answer/id #uuid "5ff6750e-5b03-5487-82ba-4c0f1e9b42e0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/eb621888b04d5bd9b842624c278422995db44097adde61bbc769e8df0a5a47e7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98525/selection/2d3b9ad4e4c74633b2b4f919cd01d62f8ce7e23fe54d1708cf8c89af86e4fbe5" :answer/id #uuid "0fbbf05b-8162-539c-8113-e859571ef9dd" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/0b6a1907f045e737c1f136a47d941069efbfd23a389add17d1ae31cf336763b8.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98525/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3ab181-a050-5268-94ed-94744cae622e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98525/selection/6193263a58aa2143a42fb9eb213c06ab78c2e89699fb0ee0761789e409f1b3f7" :answer-field/choices ["q-98525/selection/6193263a58aa2143a42fb9eb213c06ab78c2e89699fb0ee0761789e409f1b3f7" "q-98525/selection/feb719f09513e76a7ba6b8a379ebf4507d684c9bf6b01efc8b3d986d8a9bd326" "q-98525/selection/4813f5e1a1ef806016d68dc3b91f32b447fabce2bb8cf28e19c337b0d2228395" "q-98525/selection/54630732ebc6196df1a383656549cfed9cd9807944e20428ce42c8367f632cb7" "q-98525/selection/2d3b9ad4e4c74633b2b4f919cd01d62f8ce7e23fe54d1708cf8c89af86e4fbe5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98525" :question/id #uuid "519b7e3f-5e6b-5321-a66f-0a3fdc3bd943" :question/math-academy-id "q-98525" :question/problem "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" :question/worked-solution "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98525/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66924d52-fd39-5308-b78e-611e3d3129ad"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98525"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-163642"] :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-161114/selection/fbba2282d0f8beb03471ee9e51af677372e074f856fa626d26c85344c5591b6a" :answer/id #uuid "254097d6-f674-5cee-b836-1f0fb8e188ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\pi }^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161114/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "d5c05471-f12d-55e7-9901-267fe90e8ef4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161114/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "dd2024a9-31c2-5ab4-8f1b-5b3d44d87152" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161114/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "da0f71ac-ac79-53a5-8242-505aad22a02a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161114/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a0f485c8-8cd5-5a48-ab7e-35be33d34082" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161114/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cf89217-10fb-502b-bb7f-a7ab0f96783e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161114/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-161114/selection/fbba2282d0f8beb03471ee9e51af677372e074f856fa626d26c85344c5591b6a" "q-161114/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-161114/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-161114/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-161114/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161114" :question/id #uuid "7644e0cb-fe7e-5af8-a7f6-5b53f6f2ec86" :question/math-academy-id "q-161114" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/5b5b5e82eb8dca9ae71c08b306ac4618500c7c3af4aa0858297d5585e055007f.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $4$ and $6,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $4$ and $6$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ we obtain $r=4.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36,$ we obtain $r=6.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,4\\le r\\le 6,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{4}^{6}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{4}^{6}\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {[r]}_{4}^{6}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (6-4)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161114/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab96fd62-5043-501a-a3b5-5ee705f69d18"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161114"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-111083/selection/9e0ecd3ac52ff99368669f5f34617bf0346b508d0e5368e317a2e814e87142d0" :answer/id #uuid "b76e551d-542c-5953-b371-3eb6aa5c1290" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111083/selection/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" :answer/id #uuid "eaa2ed43-626c-52f6-bd69-46f64fcc1498" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111083/selection/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f" :answer/id #uuid "8718fa61-5101-5d42-8ff5-a4623b342288" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111083/selection/441f9a41e4c1d7c29719a73a7d795c394ee1337b1a990ebfb59a7b69a254418f" :answer/id #uuid "5b01b0ad-f144-5698-8cca-3d3fac82494a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111083/selection/a44ed9268297050614edc4097a04d3089044bc70b376ad604b0445193a602b0a" :answer/id #uuid "91dc9fea-f836-5738-a361-7ddb57dd111c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-111083/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "db0d6d73-e8ba-5736-8685-c62c1ec3e267" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-111083/selection/441f9a41e4c1d7c29719a73a7d795c394ee1337b1a990ebfb59a7b69a254418f" :answer-field/choices ["q-111083/selection/9e0ecd3ac52ff99368669f5f34617bf0346b508d0e5368e317a2e814e87142d0" "q-111083/selection/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" "q-111083/selection/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f" "q-111083/selection/441f9a41e4c1d7c29719a73a7d795c394ee1337b1a990ebfb59a7b69a254418f" "q-111083/selection/a44ed9268297050614edc4097a04d3089044bc70b376ad604b0445193a602b0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-111083" :question/id #uuid "698a78eb-ef71-5b2a-bf3e-b4f41a43c1f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111083" :question/problem "|  | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nCompute $P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2)$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above." :question/worked-solution "Using the values in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1\\,\\,\\text{or}\\,\\,Y<2) & =P((X,Y)\\in {(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}) \\\\  & =P((X,Y)=(0,1))+P((X,Y)=(1,1)) \\\\  & +P((X,Y)=(1,2))+P((X,Y)=(2,1)) \\\\  & =f(0,1)+f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(2,1) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.1+0.25 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-111083/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-111083"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109090/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "fa84e3a5-6f80-5b1a-8f2b-de06bf6d89be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109090/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "0cad8773-dfb7-510f-b62a-c1f0ba7a323a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109090/selection/2f11c8fed5f07b28ddc5a526ecc698b6fad690e4972d6f2e53e630e7719b9857" :answer/id #uuid "56d3a88c-7ad8-57fd-9c72-7b52d059228f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}+4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109090/selection/5dedd3fc97eb64beda5d906ad6dd87aaae34db54c1ed35ec3a4b810f0ed5b6e3" :answer/id #uuid "a3bb1365-7088-5b65-86dd-73e3bcd87292" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109090/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "10e4f492-372c-569e-ab2a-68c9e0bb25d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109090/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c7f961-8bd0-5161-8904-d148e0c79084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109090/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-109090/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-109090/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-109090/selection/2f11c8fed5f07b28ddc5a526ecc698b6fad690e4972d6f2e53e630e7719b9857" "q-109090/selection/5dedd3fc97eb64beda5d906ad6dd87aaae34db54c1ed35ec3a4b810f0ed5b6e3" "q-109090/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109090" :question/id #uuid "fac7001d-ff73-507a-96fa-7c0e89246538" :question/math-academy-id "q-109090" :question/problem "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=0,$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4].$ \n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{1}{5}$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\sqrt{5}$ and $b=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=0.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =(-1)^{2}\\cdot f(-1)+{0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}X+4] & =5\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109090/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109090"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-42567/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" :answer/id #uuid "83a6ed1d-84b6-5db6-8099-6e8d5d474f50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42567/selection/d08818d9751d48a05f01ba37497d3da27fbe70f705234d8e06d8830c6b479721" :answer/id #uuid "6393cbad-9a82-5290-abe1-d2c63b65f26d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42567/selection/57f27b4de16f64fcab2f3b810a7087fb3b270a063a6807c410b738013ce41fde" :answer/id #uuid "5bf9317b-89ed-519e-93b9-193c5f3cd387" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42567/selection/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" :answer/id #uuid "cc1b613e-f4b3-52f3-bf0c-27b40021a8c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42567/selection/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f" :answer/id #uuid "e78f7d83-8f03-50a7-98ca-15315cdaa1a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-42567/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f0c6b-6959-594d-9567-93b7821e26a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-42567/selection/57f27b4de16f64fcab2f3b810a7087fb3b270a063a6807c410b738013ce41fde" :answer-field/choices ["q-42567/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" "q-42567/selection/d08818d9751d48a05f01ba37497d3da27fbe70f705234d8e06d8830c6b479721" "q-42567/selection/57f27b4de16f64fcab2f3b810a7087fb3b270a063a6807c410b738013ce41fde" "q-42567/selection/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" "q-42567/selection/0e8c61e1cbe5b6d4b3e52c0431e48b853c5faa01ca7ca0f5f9cf780292b06b1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-42567" :question/id #uuid "c49a23bb-a7fc-57e9-921a-fc537e2e5220" :question/math-academy-id "q-42567" :question/problem "In a particular city, about $30%$ of mornings are cloudy and $40%$ of days are rainy. \nIt is known that $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings. What is the probability that a randomly selected cloudy morning continues as a rainy day?" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be the event that a randomly selected morning is cloudy, and let $R$ be the event that a randomly selected day is rainy. Then, we require $P(R|C),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(R|C)=\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)}\n$$\n\n- $P(C)=0.3,$ since $30%$ of mornings are cloudy.\n\n- $P(R)=0.4,$ since $40%$ of days are rainy.\n\n- $P(C|R)=0.6,$ since $60%$ of rainy days start with cloudy mornings.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|C) & =\\frac{P(C|R)P(R)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6\\cdot 0.4}{0.3} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-42567/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "616b7aa2-2c55-5a29-a908-545a4bad4376"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-42567"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-148669/selection/6304f42ea0e77771e89c0530ca03a2f044df3f47a23581fdeeacccc985194259" :answer/id #uuid "92f65170-b156-5f2e-8b59-42e60774b300" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.923" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-148669/selection/1c554d424381596e91e29f2904a5692bc70e7fcc3c6a501b24b1f8cb840b624f" :answer/id #uuid "ac0fd95d-e9c3-5cb1-99ca-c48ae9d97099" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.664" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-148669/selection/f6e80b1b710cee3e28dd6801d0effecdde0d0eb77ac7c9456c2233340b797410" :answer/id #uuid "73a703bd-afbc-5e88-956f-88eb56fc44ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.943" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-148669/selection/1a3f9250a23acefe56991ac01a03569f16da025ce6abb8362dfd17ea5a799a20" :answer/id #uuid "1f6f3aab-e069-585c-a5dd-dcba5897f9e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.872" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-148669/selection/840f017875680f1b419017197a05003602657595c855a4157853f68495634c6b" :answer/id #uuid "e44e619d-6352-5d7a-b131-f45b826bfe80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.885" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-148669/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2654228b-08dd-5bec-83b0-6f72a011e1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-148669/selection/1c554d424381596e91e29f2904a5692bc70e7fcc3c6a501b24b1f8cb840b624f" :answer-field/choices ["q-148669/selection/6304f42ea0e77771e89c0530ca03a2f044df3f47a23581fdeeacccc985194259" "q-148669/selection/1c554d424381596e91e29f2904a5692bc70e7fcc3c6a501b24b1f8cb840b624f" "q-148669/selection/f6e80b1b710cee3e28dd6801d0effecdde0d0eb77ac7c9456c2233340b797410" "q-148669/selection/1a3f9250a23acefe56991ac01a03569f16da025ce6abb8362dfd17ea5a799a20" "q-148669/selection/840f017875680f1b419017197a05003602657595c855a4157853f68495634c6b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-148669" :question/id #uuid "a8c2e20f-0f73-51af-a685-5cac68a35ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-148669" :question/problem "A local newspaper is sold by three distributors, $1,$ $2,$ and $3.$\n\n- Distributor $1$ gets $36%$ of the copies.\n\n- Distributor $2$ gets $23%$ of the copies, and $92%$ of those copies are sold.\n\n- Distributor $3$ gets $41%$ of the copies, and $95%$ of those copies are sold.\n\nIf $3%$ of all copies have some printing defect, $70%$ of defective copies are sold, and $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, what is the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be the event that a copy has a printing defect, $S$ be the event that a copy is sold, and ${D}_{1},{D}_{2},{D}_{3}$ be the event that a copy is given to distributor $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({D}_{1})=0.36 \\\\  & P({D}_{2})=0.23 &  & P(S|{D}_{2})=0.92 \\\\  & P({D}_{3})=0.41 &  & P(S|{D}_{3})=0.95\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${D}_{1}⊔{D}_{2}⊔{D}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({D}_{1})+P({D}_{2})+P({D}_{3})=0.36+0.23+0.41=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of all copies has some printing defect, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.03.\n$$\n\n- Since $70%$ of defective copies are sold, we have\n\n$$\nP(S|R)=0.7.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.5%$ of sold copies are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|S)=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen copy bought by distributor $1$ is sold, which is represented as $P(S|{D}_{1}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S)} \\\\ P(R|S) & =\\frac{P(S|R)P(R)}{P(S|{D}_{1})P({D}_{1})+P(S|{D}_{2})P({D}_{2})+P(S|{D}_{3})P({D}_{3})} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{(0.7)(0.03)}{P(S|{D}_{1})(0.36)+(0.92)(0.23)+(0.95)(0.41)} \\\\ 0.025 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(S|{D}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.025(0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011) & =0.021 \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{0.021}{0.025} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1})+0.6011 & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ 0.36P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011 \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.36}(\\frac{21}{25}-0.6011) \\\\ P(S|{D}_{1}) & ≈0.664\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-148669/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a4fee2d-88c3-5381-bd55-98990abc7df3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-148669"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-118552/selection/c5b7aea79f73ebc562cbcff24725697bdc076b45efa2d86abecbb1b4fa682579" :answer/id #uuid "74a3ebf5-b294-5497-baf7-ae35b4e1f967" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "600" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118552/selection/e792eba67a521531d68e5f67786d5b3cea0fc3869014543152be243f2639fb08" :answer/id #uuid "d8d8fc59-52e9-5ee5-9939-6579d13a3e20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "300" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118552/selection/0d88b864eee0ad524c86e85d3d27ed89cb05d457dfb747dbdd078831ff1ff132" :answer/id #uuid "956c66aa-326f-5a06-be61-c05003bfa3eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,200" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118552/selection/fdd7797bbc4d4bbc3d52610e034d3690d5467a84c7382cd359f243b6c7c0743f" :answer/id #uuid "8800b3d1-7cf8-51e0-944b-60d87f9f0afa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "850" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118552/selection/478fd1e25e3e34a29fea1c3ec66147c061ba20da2ebcc365831518058573cd79" :answer/id #uuid "0ca48a55-b183-5ba6-bab7-3d758f101f7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "500" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-118552/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2458073f-13ac-5d6c-aef7-398cb69aef1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-118552/selection/c5b7aea79f73ebc562cbcff24725697bdc076b45efa2d86abecbb1b4fa682579" :answer-field/choices ["q-118552/selection/c5b7aea79f73ebc562cbcff24725697bdc076b45efa2d86abecbb1b4fa682579" "q-118552/selection/e792eba67a521531d68e5f67786d5b3cea0fc3869014543152be243f2639fb08" "q-118552/selection/0d88b864eee0ad524c86e85d3d27ed89cb05d457dfb747dbdd078831ff1ff132" "q-118552/selection/fdd7797bbc4d4bbc3d52610e034d3690d5467a84c7382cd359f243b6c7c0743f" "q-118552/selection/478fd1e25e3e34a29fea1c3ec66147c061ba20da2ebcc365831518058573cd79"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-118552" :question/id #uuid "21e1d7d5-e8e8-5e32-9fd2-ba17c7038c72" :question/math-academy-id "q-118552" :question/problem "The population $y(t)$ of Weddell seals in a certain region of Antarctica follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.2y-0.002{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years. According to this model, what is the maximum possible seal population?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.2y-0.002{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002y(600-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=600.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $600,$ and we conclude that the number of seals cannot exceed $600.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-118552/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4728b3c6-a600-569d-922e-228e9f6fb2a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-118552"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139448/selection/472cab20b18245f70f27f4866c92200d0800747672169a765fb3d8fcc5cee878" :answer/id #uuid "c36ebd00-683a-5e3c-9a28-ade34577392c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139448/selection/44413af2b745b32d6360efc342431b9afb8cb83dcab172198e95d84e34a4f405" :answer/id #uuid "4836cd99-8949-51d8-b090-f062ac317564" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139448/selection/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" :answer/id #uuid "a17252a6-31dc-5d50-a563-9e0f116a754e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139448/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "75299007-da8f-58fd-a7f1-9b1339182643" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139448/selection/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" :answer/id #uuid "ff4a5a41-6436-54f6-99ed-8854f0ba75d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139448/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccc5fe84-e0eb-5c89-8ed3-a3003bceca78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139448/selection/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" :answer-field/choices ["q-139448/selection/472cab20b18245f70f27f4866c92200d0800747672169a765fb3d8fcc5cee878" "q-139448/selection/44413af2b745b32d6360efc342431b9afb8cb83dcab172198e95d84e34a4f405" "q-139448/selection/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" "q-139448/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-139448/selection/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139448" :question/id #uuid "6b76a4e9-0550-5e9b-86c9-b6dc6aa2f3d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139448" :question/problem "Which of the following is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]?\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,(A-5I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-5I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A-5I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{1}=0,$ where ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-5I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-5I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-5I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-139448/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "de97ba5b-7e05-5f3d-b11d-bf8e10c3bc54"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139448"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97078/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "794915b4-c2f3-5f27-8bf0-5af14901813f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97078/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "068c9e7e-8f1e-5353-b607-c6b3e7cbdd1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97078/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "58a11bc8-a782-5f92-8920-c066e8821e8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97078/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "389401a5-6afb-562c-9b74-51cde6742754" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97078/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5373e557-c82a-5af5-843e-9b028b00e209" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97078/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f70b5f7-3b71-5355-aff9-d8df21fbbbec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97078/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-97078/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-97078/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-97078/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-97078/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-97078/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97078" :question/id #uuid "b7b6dd53-171d-534b-b5b5-b5fe81eabd1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-97078" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97078/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97078"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-110220/selection/9e0ecd3ac52ff99368669f5f34617bf0346b508d0e5368e317a2e814e87142d0" :answer/id #uuid "91fe8502-88a9-5946-bb20-f9f217bfbc2a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110220/selection/625896633137e0044aec1ec040d8233380e151c0f7ffbedd051e7544cbdce1c4" :answer/id #uuid "e4b781f1-21d5-516f-a56f-2aba6d4471d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110220/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" :answer/id #uuid "bfbe2db0-dc18-5d7c-840a-19ecaec379f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110220/selection/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" :answer/id #uuid "208d15e2-290f-5d19-90a7-cac6761f6e29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110220/selection/22bbee0d8e9610e940e635938755e10bd27ffea1adddb9664ad643e6998d9bdf" :answer/id #uuid "f0b8edcc-96fd-5f4f-b5d1-f14e6777bf16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-110220/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "01a580a8-b326-5f5d-842f-75c055c84bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-110220/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" :answer-field/choices ["q-110220/selection/9e0ecd3ac52ff99368669f5f34617bf0346b508d0e5368e317a2e814e87142d0" "q-110220/selection/625896633137e0044aec1ec040d8233380e151c0f7ffbedd051e7544cbdce1c4" "q-110220/selection/bd0c5293c05dbcd56341c67931e9e0ae46e20e1d0de3da140e1b2dcd87ea42e2" "q-110220/selection/5d227958e8e2ab9feb7a492bded665cf59dfa9121f7d82334971c99adf799e26" "q-110220/selection/22bbee0d8e9610e940e635938755e10bd27ffea1adddb9664ad643e6998d9bdf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-110220" :question/id #uuid "499118d7-5d2d-512d-85b0-2761f7186709" :question/math-academy-id "q-110220" :question/problem "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nCalculate $P({X}^{2}=9).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's first add an ${X}^{2}$ row to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}^{2}$ | $9$ | $1$ | $1$ | $9$ | $81$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.35$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third rows of our table, we can immediately conclude that \n\n$$\nP({X}^{2}=9)=0.35+0.1=0.45.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-110220/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "18abaef5-2dc8-5961-bd2a-e6cc52cce7bb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-110220"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-163548"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070542/assets/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-105895/selection/ce7ae9e7cd823753d0659ab5518c31ee829007b48720787068b32732166969f5" :answer/id #uuid "2f605ade-6095-5f65-9446-8ebbfe1caefe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,-2,3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105895/selection/8c80ce6a2d817c401b149af5ebe7829954ddd3deef06a2f13d09364c805c2527" :answer/id #uuid "6aa0dddf-66e0-5312-ba7d-0527083a5dc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,0,-3⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105895/selection/b5f6ab52540f1afe484c3336eaf7b6f8f95f23fc172736e3138c53c9d528fea8" :answer/id #uuid "21641dfe-2aa8-592c-9a2a-0dc6991ffabc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-1,2⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105895/selection/1b3398d7447262539ae7d5e5aa3cd1ba38590b021f127cd72acbc3b68ebee67e" :answer/id #uuid "bf8b6d97-79a7-54e4-9d97-eaee97362d23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,4,0⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105895/selection/c457855895bc1067a6194394000ebd060f4468538e77f0a1b68c4b2c254c1515" :answer/id #uuid "4ac61db9-581c-5407-8871-55a536d13738" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨3,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-105895/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce8719f-c082-5922-bad1-e0173cb1e489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-105895/selection/b5f6ab52540f1afe484c3336eaf7b6f8f95f23fc172736e3138c53c9d528fea8" :answer-field/choices ["q-105895/selection/ce7ae9e7cd823753d0659ab5518c31ee829007b48720787068b32732166969f5" "q-105895/selection/8c80ce6a2d817c401b149af5ebe7829954ddd3deef06a2f13d09364c805c2527" "q-105895/selection/b5f6ab52540f1afe484c3336eaf7b6f8f95f23fc172736e3138c53c9d528fea8" "q-105895/selection/1b3398d7447262539ae7d5e5aa3cd1ba38590b021f127cd72acbc3b68ebee67e" "q-105895/selection/c457855895bc1067a6194394000ebd060f4468538e77f0a1b68c4b2c254c1515"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-105895" :question/id #uuid "b8abe39b-bb92-55f7-bb84-89cdb536b574" :question/math-academy-id "q-105895" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨-1,0,x{y}^{3}⟩$ and the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=yz.$ Given that\n\n$$\n∇\\times F=⟨3x{y}^{2},-{y}^{3},0⟩,\\,∇f=⟨0,z,y⟩,\n$$\n\ncalculate $∇\\times (f\\,F)$ at the point $(0,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We shall use the following identity:\n\n$$\n∇\\times (f\\,F)=f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $∇f\\times F.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f\\times F & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & z & y \\\\ -1 & 0 & x{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =[z(x{y}^{3})-y(0)]i-[0(x{y}^{3})-y(-1)]j+[0(0)-z(-1)]k \\\\  & =xz{y}^{3}\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F) & =f∇\\times F+∇f\\times F \\\\  & =yz⟨3x{y}^{2},-{y}^{3},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4},0⟩+⟨xz{y}^{3},-y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4xz{y}^{3},-z{y}^{4}-y,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\times (f\\,F)(0,-1,2) & =⟨4(0)(2)(-1)^{3},-2(-1)^{4}-(-1),2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-105895/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe539af-1253-5ce3-a65b-eb50ed639c52"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-105895"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-126031/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "82dbdc2f-390b-5464-afc5-a6e0401eb8e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126031/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "10c5d6ce-a270-5504-870e-d1547fa6d4bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126031/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" :answer/id #uuid "ec4c754b-eeac-5261-b97f-ae67f63753ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126031/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" :answer/id #uuid "ce84bfcc-1740-5f0a-9e05-b303c55f346b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126031/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "ea11022f-b894-57fc-8592-605dd62043ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-126031/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "15aa5794-c6e7-58c5-875f-8b6c68d5310f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-126031/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-126031/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-126031/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-126031/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" "q-126031/selection/e3b46c3e63ee8ef5a071cd8ef5f7250114c0c6983477af061861d82a06039d46" "q-126031/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-126031" :question/id #uuid "e4f38637-761c-59db-ada2-8df3d6501eff" :question/math-academy-id "q-126031" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>2)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>2) & =P(2<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{x+1}]}_{2}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-126031/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-126031"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-164831"] :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =5, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2) & ≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+6+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =11+\\frac{1}{2}(44) \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-164830/selection/9efab0db9100b4579e5f8944a18edb16a94f7f36663b6e46d66ba4d959a0a05a" :answer/id #uuid "6f64d774-b919-5dc1-b559-39561ffea51d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164830/selection/adc1efee79574765cf2c5436019beca3e2a893e55409939fc7463f5a60bcd501" :answer/id #uuid "0b4206cd-1af1-553b-8b9c-3c3c6a36b438" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164830/selection/e54bc7ef6567c18d2bd41f111e996c4093d71d1a747dee873be482a8c7d8ec86" :answer/id #uuid "f1c01546-7de0-52d2-beb7-ff217799abec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164830/selection/ab84cbfbc4c98e6d84a164c9eb226f3e46b641bc539c73db10cf1b79f4ebbe08" :answer/id #uuid "d74da73c-8972-5c48-8009-284af2146857" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164830/selection/83c00e0df43140687595bc62836772c17d0728d02952fac2c18744866093645f" :answer/id #uuid "1a11a371-8d2e-5a1d-a12d-9530631a461b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164830/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c3727fc-202d-57f2-ab46-0fca87ebaebe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-164830/selection/9efab0db9100b4579e5f8944a18edb16a94f7f36663b6e46d66ba4d959a0a05a" :answer-field/choices ["q-164830/selection/9efab0db9100b4579e5f8944a18edb16a94f7f36663b6e46d66ba4d959a0a05a" "q-164830/selection/adc1efee79574765cf2c5436019beca3e2a893e55409939fc7463f5a60bcd501" "q-164830/selection/e54bc7ef6567c18d2bd41f111e996c4093d71d1a747dee873be482a8c7d8ec86" "q-164830/selection/ab84cbfbc4c98e6d84a164c9eb226f3e46b641bc539c73db10cf1b79f4ebbe08" "q-164830/selection/83c00e0df43140687595bc62836772c17d0728d02952fac2c18744866093645f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-164830" :question/id #uuid "8b9a0044-c09a-5774-bb14-17d82777ff05" :question/math-academy-id "q-164830" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =0, & \\,{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =0, & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & ≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ -(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(1-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-164830/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164830"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-73257/selection/0514cbde4dbcd9525e8eb1c7c1d8459bbe8b222f9a1040cbe8674975a3360748" :answer/id #uuid "da666821-a92c-58e8-ae5c-e9ca2f31d8c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2/3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73257/selection/3cce3c6992a0cb806829e32c775cce64178dfb295abe34276fd90cdbaa548b5b" :answer/id #uuid "77809515-85bf-5517-861f-53ef115df517" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{1/2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3/2}=1." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73257/selection/a0bd6c6136694cb538fa155d73283866ccaec0485eed6342d98616a56bac145a" :answer/id #uuid "4c0fe807-be82-513c-af75-0c010b3822a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73257/selection/ed4280be6dc3dff6e2b3fbd9e554ce3604d7429b2c2d6f355c7ab9666118cf8f" :answer/id #uuid "f20531fc-cf32-5341-9c48-ff9deeae35c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{1/2}=1." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73257/selection/30dfa40f3282c9cc924967bd57197f20714b3c2f9e4b943b58634901df999580" :answer/id #uuid "53b1eaf4-603b-5176-9a44-cc72731259da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2/3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/2}=1." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73257/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd3832a9-d2dd-5ba0-87a9-c9aee6a7039e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-73257/selection/a0bd6c6136694cb538fa155d73283866ccaec0485eed6342d98616a56bac145a" :answer-field/choices ["q-73257/selection/0514cbde4dbcd9525e8eb1c7c1d8459bbe8b222f9a1040cbe8674975a3360748" "q-73257/selection/3cce3c6992a0cb806829e32c775cce64178dfb295abe34276fd90cdbaa548b5b" "q-73257/selection/a0bd6c6136694cb538fa155d73283866ccaec0485eed6342d98616a56bac145a" "q-73257/selection/ed4280be6dc3dff6e2b3fbd9e554ce3604d7429b2c2d6f355c7ab9666118cf8f" "q-73257/selection/30dfa40f3282c9cc924967bd57197f20714b3c2f9e4b943b58634901df999580"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-73257" :question/id #uuid "33ec3330-aeaf-5e9b-a359-02e080019320" :question/math-academy-id "q-73257" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the plane $z-y=3$ onto the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y=3\\,⟹\\,z=y+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{3{y}^{2}+6y+9}{12} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}+2y+3}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{({y}^{2}+2y+1)+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}+2}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{3/2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{2}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-73257/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-73257"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-343302"] :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=2.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=2,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-338767/selection/2fbf6e648ff9254f716d85c8f2eda2853108e06983eb068670b37a49488bc26e" :answer/id #uuid "94d95791-2cc7-5a8b-962f-73c236a5c448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338767/selection/94cb15144feaa0cbb30df73a75b525b4529c17b0b49651cf10e8282d84e09be9" :answer/id #uuid "c11e43fe-ba7a-53c0-90d7-a59aa3b96b03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338767/selection/45de6c737cc20dcd53917cf87df582d4ace904aa5a7d32a8cc4f6ac60cb22770" :answer/id #uuid "bc64cc80-c370-594d-8ddb-00cdb5c8bcb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338767/selection/3b97a0e9fc5771aa15a4aab7e679c4000bf766ed0d4eab40f797d2ddddb388db" :answer/id #uuid "de4cbfee-dc26-5f53-9de8-a54d1fed0a56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338767/selection/6605d7b0542b83f3e7a43a7643e24729f9cb578832e4a1723978fa5c0c98d589" :answer/id #uuid "5bbc4f3e-ef1a-51f8-837a-6b1ed0325ebf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x{e}^{2x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338767/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "01fdd2a3-162b-5f4d-bd71-bfbd4cb30ce6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-338767/selection/6605d7b0542b83f3e7a43a7643e24729f9cb578832e4a1723978fa5c0c98d589" :answer-field/choices ["q-338767/selection/2fbf6e648ff9254f716d85c8f2eda2853108e06983eb068670b37a49488bc26e" "q-338767/selection/94cb15144feaa0cbb30df73a75b525b4529c17b0b49651cf10e8282d84e09be9" "q-338767/selection/45de6c737cc20dcd53917cf87df582d4ace904aa5a7d32a8cc4f6ac60cb22770" "q-338767/selection/3b97a0e9fc5771aa15a4aab7e679c4000bf766ed0d4eab40f797d2ddddb388db" "q-338767/selection/6605d7b0542b83f3e7a43a7643e24729f9cb578832e4a1723978fa5c0c98d589"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338767" :question/id #uuid "cb7b8734-1edc-5c36-87f9-ef5befb2dd32" :question/math-academy-id "q-338767" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+5y=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following results (integration constants omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=x{e}^{2x}.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}-({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{′}({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-(2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{e}^{4x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)({e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(x{cos}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+x{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =x{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\,(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+{{x{e}^{2x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338767/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338767"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-338142/selection/faa7782239160b27e4d74cddee25059fa40603550cdbd6585116b498411882bb" :answer/id #uuid "4e3d0790-793b-5370-982b-173ea365929b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338142/selection/0bb643afc84be5588dec04f10e18553e99c989f8e69d5be46e5a82628b6cb7af" :answer/id #uuid "93a524bc-dbe0-527a-9025-3d60ee66662c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338142/selection/8d7363e24edc20ca7b8d4e86723ecf763ece0f2ce3a9e734d08f4acd9985ac46" :answer/id #uuid "2263fea8-99ab-5528-9114-c1a5f4d13e64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338142/selection/d6f1ea0cd2b5b3d959787a2f40de752b7a8540c51fb43e7571a534fa25479c6d" :answer/id #uuid "dfbfd963-2906-5722-b152-e3c6726d4626" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338142/selection/87807a0159f01aa57f83734b81e6b93785ce920df6f6515f90bbf5789461ca52" :answer/id #uuid "4f701a29-414c-5242-af93-500e7082a757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338142/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "210210bc-cd82-5454-b1dc-84e092e994aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-338142/selection/8d7363e24edc20ca7b8d4e86723ecf763ece0f2ce3a9e734d08f4acd9985ac46" :answer-field/choices ["q-338142/selection/faa7782239160b27e4d74cddee25059fa40603550cdbd6585116b498411882bb" "q-338142/selection/0bb643afc84be5588dec04f10e18553e99c989f8e69d5be46e5a82628b6cb7af" "q-338142/selection/8d7363e24edc20ca7b8d4e86723ecf763ece0f2ce3a9e734d08f4acd9985ac46" "q-338142/selection/d6f1ea0cd2b5b3d959787a2f40de752b7a8540c51fb43e7571a534fa25479c6d" "q-338142/selection/87807a0159f01aa57f83734b81e6b93785ce920df6f6515f90bbf5789461ca52"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-338142" :question/id #uuid "f6d64847-1769-5602-97fa-762ad6861828" :question/math-academy-id "q-338142" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be written as\n\n$$\ny(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+g(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants. Which of the following could be the function $g(x)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the variation of parameters method. You may also wish to make use of the following result (integration constant omitted):*\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear differential equation with linearly independent fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ Then, according to the method of variation of parameters, we can seek a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ using the ansatz\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}=f(x) \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x)=-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\\,{u}_{2}(x)=\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx,\n$$\n\nwhere $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})$ is the Wronskian.\n\nFirst, we note that the forcing term is $f(x)=4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x.$\n\nFrom the given information, we see that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental solutions to the corresponding homogeneous equation are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe can use the method of variation of parameters if these solutions are linearly independent. So, let's first compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(x){y}_{2}^{′}(x)-{y}_{1}^{′}(x){y}_{2}(x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)^{′}-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{′}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)-({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{cos}^{2}⁡2x-{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2{e}^{2x}{sin}^{2}⁡2x \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}({cos}^{2}⁡2x+{sin}^{2}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are independent.\n\nLet’s compute ${u}_{1}(x)$ and ${u}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}(x) & =-\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{2}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =-2\\int dx \\\\  & =-2x \\\\ {u}_{2}(x) & =\\int \\frac{f(x){y}_{1}(x)}{W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}⁡2x\\cdot {e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{2{e}^{2x}}\\,dx \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}(x) & ={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2x)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|)({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution is the sum of the complementary and particular solutions, and can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x){e}^{x}+{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}}_{⏟}}_{g(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ng(x)=(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x|-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x){e}^{x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338142/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b7c316a-2ec9-5f4c-853c-e82b09755db0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338142"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343739/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "eaa8fa5c-f847-59f7-bdde-4368b38f88f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343739/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "3c0dbd04-0acd-5318-b5ba-53d0564d4a34" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343739/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "f0dae42c-61ff-5b67-bdf3-3cef1606a783" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343739/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "da78d2ec-409f-5514-8467-a0e722e40c14" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343739/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d52a12f7-7409-52db-9689-0c43c46a9fe6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343739/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "77da9420-ee03-5ff5-a08e-1c47ca45eb35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-343739/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-343739/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-343739/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-343739/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-343739/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-343739/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343739" :question/id #uuid "db727116-3b09-56e5-8d24-96a8c59d2a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-343739" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=-1$ and $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343739/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343739"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-343761/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "30dcb20d-e5bc-50aa-a16d-c0cb4a88e0e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343761/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5be7ba38-0784-532d-823b-79721c845b8c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343761/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "1632a717-fa9b-5a41-b58e-6cbba7f3e9bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343761/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "5f17327b-1293-5d02-9614-56cb5bc32a65" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343761/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "dca16548-7f00-5f60-a5f4-041400b2a11a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-343761/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7490e5a-e807-53a6-b133-4feed29aa08b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-343761/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-343761/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-343761/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-343761/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-343761/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-343761/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-343761" :question/id #uuid "512495a2-8bfb-52b3-bf2e-01e8a787e6fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343761" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n- ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =-12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $D=-2.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n- $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-343761/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cac41120-247d-5821-b7f6-b342dbcf8f18"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-343761"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
